The ATmega2560 register names are from `<avr/io.h>`. Set the desired waveform/counting modes independently. The clock-select commands preserve the other control bits.

```cpp
// (i) Toggle the PC3 output latch.
DDRC |= (1<<PC3);
PORTC ^= (1<<PC3);

// (ii) Timer5, internal clock divided by 64: CS52:0 = 011.
TCCR5B = (TCCR5B & ~((1<<CS52) | (1<<CS51) | (1<<CS50)))
       | (1<<CS51) | (1<<CS50);

// (iii) Timer1, external rising edges on T1 (PD6): CS12:0 = 111.
DDRD &= ~(1<<PD6);
TCCR1B |= (1<<CS12) | (1<<CS11) | (1<<CS10);

// (iv) Internal 2.56 V reference: REFS1:0 = 11.
ADMUX |= (1<<REFS1) | (1<<REFS0);

// (v) Left-adjust the ADC result.
ADMUX |= (1<<ADLAR);
```

Use the chip's actual T1 pin. `ICES1` selects the separate input-capture edge. For the internal reference, use the AREF bypass capacitor and leave any external reference voltage disconnected. Allow the reference to settle. [Microchip ATmega2560 datasheet, ADMUX register](https://ww1.microchip.com/downloads/en/devicedoc/atmega640-1280-1281-2560-2561-datasheet-ds40002211a.pdf#page=281).

Follow the timing pattern in tutorial 19. Take a timestamp `ti = micros()` before the instructions and subtract it from a second timestamp after the block. Print `tc` to the Serial Monitor after measuring.

This example repeats two observable register operations 200 times. The measured interval includes loop and timestamp overhead. Divide by 200 for an average per repetition, or measure a matching empty loop and subtract its overhead for a closer estimate. On the 16 MHz Mega, `micros()` advances in 4 µs steps. For a fixed assembly sequence, count its instruction cycles and divide by 16 MHz.

```cpp
unsigned long ti, tc;
unsigned int k;

void setup() {
  DDRA |= (1<<4);
  Serial.begin(9600);
}

void loop()
{
  ti = micros();
  for (k = 0; k < 200; k++) {
    PORTA |= (1<<4);
    PORTA &= ~(1<<4);
  }
  tc = micros() - ti;
  Serial.print("Time for 200 repetitions = ");
  Serial.print(tc);
  Serial.println(" us");
  delay(1000);
}
```

Assume active-high outputs, a 5 V Mega 2560 and a common ground. Use the lecture's direct-source connection for the low-current LED and low-side NPN driver for the higher-current LED. PB4 is D10 and PB5 is D11.

```text
PB4 / D10 ── Rlow ──── low-current LED ── GND

 +5 V ────── Rhigh ─── high-current LED ── collector
PB5 / D11 ── Rbase ─── base                 NPN
                                          emitter ── GND
```

Size the series resistors for the actual forward voltages and desired currents. For example, $V_F\approx2\,\mathrm V$ and 1 mA gives $R_\mathrm{low}\approx3\,\mathrm{k\Omega}$. For 10 mA, with a saturated transistor dropping about 0.2 V, $R_\mathrm{high}\approx280\,\Omega$ (300 or 330 Ω is suitable for this example). A 3.9 kΩ base resistor supplies about 1.1 mA at $V_{BE}\approx0.7\,\mathrm V$. Verify the selected device's drive and saturation ratings. The base resistor limits GPIO current.

```cpp
#define lowLed ((1<<PB4))
#define highLed ((1<<PB5))
#define ledMask (lowLed | highLed)

void setup() {
    PORTB &= ~ledMask;
    DDRB |= ledMask;
}

void loop() {
    PORTB = (PORTB & ~ledMask) | lowLed;
    delay(500);
    PORTB = (PORTB & ~ledMask) | highLed;
    delay(500);
}
```

Each update sets one LED bit and clears the other without changing the remaining port bits.

Assume a 16 MHz Mega 2560. Use PH3 (D6) as a GPIO output and adapt the Timer3 polling routine from the notes to Timer4.

A 10 Hz waveform has a 100 ms period. An 80% duty cycle needs 80 ms high and 20 ms low. Use Timer4 in CTC mode with prescaler 64, giving a 4 µs tick.

| Interval | Timer ticks | OCR4A |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| 80 ms high | 20,000 | 19,999 |
| 20 ms low | 5,000 | 4,999 |

Set PH3 high, run the compare-delay routine, then clear PH3 and call it for the low interval. The timer gives the nominal delays. The GPIO writes and timer setup add small overhead.

```cpp
#define outPin 3  // PH3

void T4delay(unsigned int compare);

void setup() {
  PORTH &= ~(1<<outPin);
  DDRH |= (1<<outPin);
  TCCR4B = 0x00;
  TCCR4A = 0x00;
  TIMSK4 = 0x00;
}

void loop()
{
  PORTH |= (1<<outPin);
  T4delay(19999);
  PORTH &= ~(1<<outPin);
  T4delay(4999);
}

void T4delay(unsigned int compare)
{
  OCR4AH = compare >> 8;
  OCR4AL = compare & 0xFF;
  TCNT4H = 0x00;
  TCNT4L = 0x00;
  TIFR4 = (1<<OCF4A);  // Clear a pending flag by writing 1
  TCCR4B = 0x0B;        // CTC, clock / 64

  while ((TIFR4 & (1<<OCF4A)) == 0) {}
  TCCR4B = 0x00;
  TIFR4 = (1<<OCF4A);
}
```

Use **ADC2 (PF2/A2)**, 5 V AVCC as the reference, the usual AREF bypass capacitor and a shared ground. Keep the analog input between ground and AVCC. A divider of 64 gives $16\,\mathrm{MHz}/64=250\,\mathrm{kHz}$, so `ADPS2:0=110`.

Set `ADLAR=1`, so `ADCH` holds result bits 9–2 for the eight-bit `PORTC`. The requested clock is above the recommended range for maximum 10-bit resolution. [ATmega2560 datasheet, ADC prescaling and conversion timing](https://ww1.microchip.com/downloads/en/devicedoc/atmega640-1280-1281-2560-2561-datasheet-ds40002211a.pdf#page=271).

```cpp
void setup() {
    PORTC = 0;
    DDRC = 0xFF;
    DDRF &= ~(1<<PF2);
    PORTF &= ~(1<<PF2);
    DIDR0 |= (1<<ADC2D);

    ADCSRB = 0x00;  // MUX5 = 0, software triggering.
    ADMUX = 0x62;
    ADCSRA = 0x86 | (1<<ADIF);  // Clear any old completion flag.
}

void loop() {
    ADCSRA |= (1<<ADSC);
    while ((ADCSRA & (1<<ADIF)) == 0) {}
    PORTC = ADCH;
    ADCSRA = 0x86 | (1<<ADIF);  // Clear ADIF by writing one.
}
```

This adapts the notes' ADC polling example to channel 2, a different prescaler and an eight-bit output port.

**Assumptions.** The question mentions three switches but specifies two input pins and four states. Use two externally pulled-down switches for PB5/PB4, with a closed switch reading 1. Generate one active-high pulse on PC3 at startup and after each stable change of selector value. Assume 16 MHz and exclusive use of Timers 1 and 3.

In CTC the compare interval is $(\mathrm{OCRnA}+1)N/f_{\mathrm{CPU}}$. For one second:

| PB5:PB4 | Timer | Prescaler | CS bits | OCRnA |
| :---: | :---: | ---: | :---: | ---: |
| 00 | 1 | 256 | 100 | 62499 |
| 01 | 1 | 1024 | 101 | 15624 |
| 10 | 3 | 256 | 100 | 62499 |
| 11 | 3 | 1024 | 101 | 15624 |

```cpp
#define pulsePin 3   // PC3

unsigned char z, next_z, last_z = 0xFF;

void T1delay(unsigned int compare, unsigned char control);
void T3delay(unsigned int compare, unsigned char control);

void setup() {
  DDRB &= ~0x30;                  // PB5 and PB4 as inputs
  PORTB &= ~0x30;                 // Use external pull-downs
  PORTC &= ~(1<<pulsePin);
  DDRC |= (1<<pulsePin);
  TCCR1B = 0x00;
  TCCR3B = 0x00;
  TCCR1A = 0x00;
  TCCR3A = 0x00;
  TIMSK1 = 0x00;
  TIMSK3 = 0x00;
}

void loop()
{
  z = (PINB & 0x30) >> 4;
  if (z != last_z) {
    delay(20);
    next_z = (PINB & 0x30) >> 4;
    if (z == next_z) {
      switch (z) {
        case 0: T1delay(62499, 0x0C); break;  // CTC, /256
        case 1: T1delay(15624, 0x0D); break;  // CTC, /1024
        case 2: T3delay(62499, 0x0C); break;
        case 3: T3delay(15624, 0x0D); break;
      }
      last_z = z;
    }
  }
}

void T1delay(unsigned int compare, unsigned char control)
{
  OCR1AH = compare >> 8;
  OCR1AL = compare & 0xFF;
  TCNT1H = 0x00;
  TCNT1L = 0x00;
  TIFR1 = (1<<OCF1A);

  PORTC |= (1<<pulsePin);
  TCCR1B = control;
  while ((TIFR1 & (1<<OCF1A)) == 0) {}

  PORTC &= ~(1<<pulsePin);
  TCCR1B = 0x00;
  TIFR1 = (1<<OCF1A);
}

void T3delay(unsigned int compare, unsigned char control)
{
  OCR3AH = compare >> 8;
  OCR3AL = compare & 0xFF;
  TCNT3H = 0x00;
  TCNT3L = 0x00;
  TIFR3 = (1<<OCF3A);

  PORTC |= (1<<pulsePin);
  TCCR3B = control;
  while ((TIFR3 & (1<<OCF3A)) == 0) {}

  PORTC &= ~(1<<pulsePin);
  TCCR3B = 0x00;
  TIFR3 = (1<<OCF3A);
}
```

PC3 is software-driven. The timer sets a nominal one-second interval with small software edge latency. The counts are derived independently for each exam prescaler.

Use **ADC0/PF0/A0**, **ADC1/PF1/A1** and **ADC3/PF3/A3** with low-impedance sources and a shared ground. Assume a 16 MHz Mega 2560, a 5 V AVCC reference with an AREF bypass capacitor and a divider of 128 for 125 kHz. Send the upper eight bits from left-adjusted `ADCH` to `PORTA`.

Use active-high LEDs with series resistors on PB2, PB3 and PB4. Turn off the other two whenever the selected channel's result is displayed.

```cpp
#define adcPins ((1<<PF0) | (1<<PF1) | (1<<PF3))
#define ledMask ((1<<PB2) | (1<<PB3) | (1<<PB4))
const unsigned char channels[] = {0, 1, 3};
const unsigned char ledBits[] = {(1<<PB2), (1<<PB3), (1<<PB4)};

unsigned char convert() {
    ADCSRA |= (1<<ADSC);
    while ((ADCSRA & (1<<ADIF)) == 0) {}
    unsigned char sample = ADCH;
    ADCSRA = 0x87 | (1<<ADIF);
    return sample;
}

void setup() {
    DDRF &= ~adcPins;
    PORTF &= ~adcPins;
    DIDR0 |= (1<<ADC0D) | (1<<ADC1D) | (1<<ADC3D);
    PORTA = 0;
    DDRA = 0xFF;
    PORTB &= ~ledMask;
    DDRB |= ledMask;

    ADCSRB = 0x00;  // MUX5 = 0 for channels 0 to 7.
    ADMUX = 0x60;
    ADCSRA = 0x87 | (1<<ADIF);
}

void loop() {
    for (unsigned char i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {
        ADMUX = (1<<REFS0) | (1<<ADLAR) | channels[i];
        (void)convert();  // Extra settling sample after changing channel.
        PORTA = convert();
        PORTB = (PORTB & ~ledMask) | ledBits[i];
        delay(250);       // Keep this channel's LED visibly illuminated.
    }
}
```

The order is ADC0/PB2, ADC1/PB3, ADC3/PB4, then repeat. Shorten the display dwell if a higher update rate is required.
